# What never belongs in an image of this backend.
#
# The first block is the one that matters: `careerguide.db` holds real conversations,
# and a `docker build` that swept it in would ship a family's chat history inside an
# image layer — where deleting it from the working tree afterwards changes nothing,
# because the layer still holds it and anyone with the image can read it.

.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

*.db
*.db-journal
*.db.bak
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
careerguide.db*

# Generated speech from when audio files were written to disk. The app now returns
# speech as a data URL and writes nothing here, but the exclusion stays so an old
# working-tree `media/` directory can never ride into an image — it is private by the
# same argument as the database.
media/

# Where `scripts/backup_db.sh` writes by default. A custom-format dump is the whole
# database in one file, and the default destination is inside this build context, so
# without this line `docker build ./backend` would put every child's answers into an
# image layer — where the file is readable by anyone who can pull the image, long after
# it was deleted from the working tree.
backups/
*.dump

# Local secrets. The container is given its environment at run time; an image that
# carries a `.env` carries it to everyone who can pull it.
.env
.env.*

# The pristine create-next-app history parked beside the project, and anything else
# version-control-shaped.
.git/
.gitignore
**/.git-scaffold-backup/

# Not needed at runtime, and large.
*.log
.pytest_cache/
.vercel/
next-env.d.ts
